---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import ProjectFooter from '../../components/ProjectFooter.astro';
import SnowboundStudy from '../../components/SnowboundStudy.astro';
const description = 'Snowballs, a freezing snow gun, and partner throwing: gameplay programming and shared level design for a two-player platformer made in two days.';
const mechanics = [
  {number:'01', title:'Snowball', subtitle:'Build with snow', slug:'snowball', duration:'5 SEC', caption:'Use a snowball as a step to climb the lower route.', text:'Leave a snowball on a pressure switch to keep a route open for your partner, or use it as a step to reach higher ground. Pushing snow also moves the character faster. Snowballs eventually disappear; larger ones last longer, creating a limited window for the next move.', implementation:'While growing, the ball’s scale and position update together to stay aligned with the character. Releasing it detaches the ball and starts a countdown based on its radius.'},
  {number:'02', title:'Snow Gun', subtitle:'Control the timing', slug:'snow-gun-freeze', duration:'8 SEC', caption:'Shoot moving platforms to control the crossing.', text:'Shoot switches from a distance and freeze moving platforms to create a usable crossing. Another shot restores movement. One player can control the timing while the other concentrates on jumping between platforms.', implementation:'A hit pauses platform movement; another resumes it, using Rigidbody type changes and the movement script.'},
  {number:'03', title:'Partner Throw', subtitle:'Launch your partner', slug:'partner-throw', duration:'8 SEC', caption:'Aim and launch a partner onto the upper route.', text:'Pick up your partner, adjust the angle, and launch them toward a ledge they cannot reach from the ground. Sending one player ahead creates a new position from which they can help open the way for the other.', implementation:'Pickup pauses the small character’s control, physics simulation, and collision. Launch restores simulation and collision, then applies an initial velocity. Landing restores normal control.'},
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><BaseHead title="Snowbound" description={description} /></head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<ProjectHeader />
<main id="main">
<section class="hero wrap">
  <div class="kicker"><span>GAMEPLAY PROGRAMMING &amp; GAME DESIGN</span><span>48-HOUR GAME JAM</span></div>
  <p class="eyebrow">TWO-PLAYER CO-OP PLATFORMER</p>
  <h1>Snowbound</h1>
  <p class="hero-subtitle">A snowy adventure. Made for two.</p>
  <div class="hero-copy"><p>Grow snowballs, freeze moving objects, and throw your partner across the snow. Built by a three-person team around <strong>a theme centered on two-player cooperation.</strong></p></div>
  <figure><img src="/projects/snowbound/cover.webp" width="1280" height="672" alt="Two players on separate snowy routes, with a snowball beside a tall obstacle" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption>SNOWBOUND / GAME JAM BUILD</figcaption></figure>
  <dl class="metadata"><div><dt>ROLE</dt><dd>Programmer · Game Designer</dd></div><div><dt>TEAM</dt><dd>3 people</dd></div><div><dt>DURATION</dt><dd>2 days</dd></div><div><dt>TOOLS</dt><dd>Unity · C# · Platformer template</dd></div></dl>
</section>
<nav class="chapters" aria-label="Project sections"><div class="wrap"><a href="#contribution">00 / Contribution</a><a href="#mechanics">01 / Mechanics</a><a href="#levels">02 / Level design</a><a href="#debugging">03 / Reflection</a></div></nav>
<section id="contribution" class="section wrap split">
<header><p class="eyebrow">00 / MY CONTRIBUTION</p><h2>Three mechanics.<br />Shared possibilities.</h2><p>I implemented the snowball, snow gun, and partner-throwing mechanics, and collaborated with my teammates on level design.</p></header>
<div class="panel"><p class="eyebrow">EXPLORING THE THEME</p><h3>What makes two players need each other?</h3><p>We explored one player controlling two characters, dividing inputs between players, and tasks across split screens. We chose a shared-screen platform puzzler, then developed cooperation through complementary abilities: one player changes the space or timing so the other can move.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="mechanics" class="tinted"><div class="section wrap"><header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / COOPERATIVE MECHANICS</p><h2>Shape the space.<br />Change the next move.</h2><dl class="abilities"><div class="role-card role-a"><img src="/projects/snowbound/character-a.webp" width="192" height="192" alt="Small snow-gun character from the game build" loading="lazy" /><div><dt><span class="role-label a">A</span> SMALL CHARACTER</dt><dd>Snow Gun</dd></div></div><div class="role-card role-b"><img src="/projects/snowbound/character-b.webp" width="192" height="192" alt="Large snowball and throwing character from the game build" loading="lazy" /><div><dt><span class="role-label b">B</span> LARGE CHARACTER</dt><dd>Snowball + Partner Throw</dd></div></div></dl></header>
<div class="mechanics">{mechanics.map(({number,title,subtitle,slug,duration,caption,text,implementation}) => <article>
  <div class="mechanic-copy"><p class="eyebrow">{number} / {subtitle}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p><p class="implementation-note">{implementation}</p></div>
  <figure><video controls muted playsinline preload="none" poster={`/projects/snowbound/${slug}.webp`} aria-label={`${title} gameplay demonstration`}><source src={`/projects/snowbound/${slug}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" /></video><figcaption>{duration} / {caption}</figcaption></figure>
</article>)}</div>
</div></section>

<section id="levels" class="section wrap">
<header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / SHARED LEVEL DESIGN</p><h2>From layout to interaction.</h2><p>We designed levels together. These original planning diagrams explore separated routes, stepped terrain, moving elements, and obstacles within the same screen. They show how we arranged the space around the characters’ abilities before and alongside implementation.</p></header>
<p class="diagram-key"><span><span class="role-label a">A</span> Snow Gun</span><span><span class="role-label b">B</span> Snowball + Partner Throw</span><span>1–3 / Action order</span></p>
<article class="level-case">
  <header><p class="eyebrow">CASE 01 / MECHANIC INTRODUCTION</p><h3>Learning to Build with Snow</h3><p>Upper and lower routes introduce using snowballs as footholds.</p></header>
  <figure><SnowboundStudy study="stairs" /><figcaption>EARLY LAYOUT / STARTS A &amp; B · CLIMBS 1–2 · REGROUP 3</figcaption></figure>
  <ol class="sequence"><li><strong>1 / Build the foothold</strong><p>B grows and releases a snowball beside the first tall step.</p></li><li><strong>2 / Climb before it disappears</strong><p>Use the ball as extra height, then repeat at the next rise. A advances along the upper route.</p></li><li><strong>3 / Meet on the right</strong><p>The routes meet on the right after B practices making usable terrain.</p></li></ol>
  <figure class="case-frame"><img src="/projects/snowbound/snowball-step.webp" width="1280" height="672" loading="lazy" alt="The snowball character using a snowball as a foothold beside the tall final stair while the partner waits above" /><figcaption>RECORDED BUILD / 01:18 — B STANDS ON THE SNOWBALL TO REACH THE NEXT STEP</figcaption></figure>
  <p class="design-note"><strong>Design value:</strong> Height gives snowball growth a concrete purpose. Size changes the usefulness of the foothold and the time available to use it.</p>
</article>
<article class="level-case">
  <header><p class="eyebrow">CASE 02 / LAUNCH, THEN SUPPORT</p><h3>Send one player ahead. Open the next route.</h3><p>Partner throwing and a snowball pressure mechanism combine to open a route through the level.</p></header>
  <figure><SnowboundStudy study="launch" /><div class="switch-key"><span><strong>3 / Pink rectangle</strong> Snowball pressure switch</span></div><figcaption>EARLY LAYOUT / 1 PICKUP &amp; AIM · 2 UPPER LANDING · 3 PRESSURE MECHANISM</figcaption></figure>
  <ol class="sequence"><li><strong>1 / Pick up and aim</strong><p>B picks up A beneath the ledge and chooses the launch angle.</p></li><li><strong>2 / Reach the upper route</strong><p>The throw gets A onto the ledge while B remains below.</p></li><li><strong>3 / Support from below</strong><p>B leaves a snowball on the lower pressure switch to move the barrier for A. The temporary ball creates a crossing window.</p></li></ol>
  <figure class="case-frame"><img src="/projects/snowbound/upper-route.webp" width="1280" height="672" loading="lazy" alt="The snow-gun player on the upper walkway while the snowball player operates the pressure mechanism at the right-hand column below" /><figcaption>RECORDED BUILD / 01:50 — UPPER AND LOWER ACTIONS CHANGE THE SAME ROUTE</figcaption></figure>
  <p class="design-note"><strong>From sketch to build:</strong> The early sketch places the pressure mechanism near the start; the final build moves it to the right-hand column.</p>
</article>
<details class="archive"><summary>More original level sketches</summary><p>Additional original sketches, retained as design process material.</p><div class="plan-grid">{[1,4,5,6,7,8,9,10].map((n) => <figure><a href={`/projects/snowbound/plan-${n}.webp`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label={`Enlarge level planning diagram ${n}`}><img src={`/projects/snowbound/plan-${n}.webp`} width="1100" height="629" loading="lazy" alt={`Original level planning diagram ${n}`} /></a><figcaption>LAYOUT STUDY / {String(n).padStart(2,'0')}</figcaption></figure>)}</div></details>
</section>
<section id="debugging" class="tinted"><div class="section wrap split">
<header><p class="eyebrow">03 / A DEVELOPMENT LESSON</p><h2>Standing still.<br />Unable to jump.</h2></header>
<div><p>A character could stand on a moving platform but could not jump. This exposed an integration problem between platform support and the template’s grounded condition: being carried by a trigger did not count as being grounded, because the template’s ground detection ignores triggers.</p><p>The implementation separates those responsibilities: a trigger carries the character by the platform’s displacement each physics step, while a separate non-trigger collider provides the surface detected by the template’s ground check, which gates jumping.</p><p>The experience taught me to understand a template’s collision, ground detection, and control logic before extending its movement rules.</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="credits wrap" aria-labelledby="credits-heading"><h2 id="credits-heading">Credits &amp; References</h2><p>Characters are inspired by Nunu &amp; Willump from <em>League of Legends</em>; the original characters belong to Riot Games. Character artwork was generated with AI using those designs as references. The prototype was built on a Unity platformer template.</p></section>
</main>
<ProjectFooter />
</body>
</html>
